<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Commons of the United Kingdom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Commons is the elected lower house of the Parliament of the United Kingdom, meeting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-westminster">Palace of Westminster</a>, to which the government is responsible. Its members are chosen by constituencies, and its control of taxation and supply made it the dominant chamber. In 1903 its resolution on the Congo led the government to send <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-casement">Roger Casement</a> to investigate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-commons-of-the-united-kingdom.jpg" alt="House of Commons of the United Kingdom" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Swapnil1101 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_House_of_Commons.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1903 the <b>British House of Commons</b> called for action, and Roger Casement, sent to investigate the upper Congo, published his report in February 1904.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
